<title>USA: U.S. Nov construction spending rose 1.9 pct.</title>
<headline>U.S. Nov construction spending rose 1.9 pct.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-01-03</dateline>
<text>
<p>U.S. Commerce Department seasonally adjusted construction spending, with comparisons.</p>
<p>    PERCENT CHANGE:   Nov     Oct--Rev from   Nov96/95</p>
<p> Total Spending	 1.9     1.5     1.8	  7.7</p>
<p>    ANNUAL RATES, BILLIONS OF U.S. DOLLARS:   Nov'95</p>
<p> Total Spending     592.0   581.0   581.2	549.7</p>
<p> Private Spending   441.6   434.3   431.1	411.0</p>
<p> -Residential	 247.6   244.6   243.9	239.9</p>
<p> --New Housing	176.3   175.6   174.9	166.4</p>
<p> ---Single-Family   155.7   156.1   155.2	148.0</p>
<p> ---Multi-Family     20.6    19.5    19.7	 18.4</p>
<p>ANNUAL RATES, BILLIONS OF U.S. DOLLARS:</p>
<p> -			 Nov	Oct--Rev from   Nov'95</p>
<p> -Nonresidential    152.3   149.9   148.8	134.7</p>
<p> --Industrial	  30.9    33.0    33.1	 32.0</p>
<p> --Office		28.4    28.1    27.1	 23.2</p>
<p> Public Spending    150.3   146.7   150.1	138.7</p>
<p>    PERCENT CHANGES:  Nov     Oct--Rev from   Nov96/95</p>
<p> Private Spending     1.7     1.4     0.9	  7.4</p>
<p> Public Spending	2.5     1.9     4.3	  8.4</p>
<p>Construction spending in 1992 dollars, which discounts the effects of inflation:Z   Percent Changes --  Nov     Oct--Rev from</p>
<p>  Total Spending	1.7     1.4     1.7</p>
<p>Annual Rates, Billions of U.S. Dollars --</p>
<p>  Total Spending    512.2   503.4   502.5</p>
<p>The department revised September construction spending to a 1.3 percent rise from a 1.2 percent increase.</p>
<p>It revised September private construction spending to a 0.4 percent increase from up 0.3 percent. September public spending was revised to up 4.3 percent from a 4.4 percent rise.  </p>
<p>The department said the 1.9 percent increase in November construction spending was the largest since March when it rose 2.3 percent.</p>
</text>
